By default the build uses the prebuilt tools/tcc/tcc.o (see the top-level
Makefile: the tools/tcc/tcc.o rule falls back to it when no tinycc/ source
tree is present). To change tanja-tcc-main.c or tanja-libc.c, build tcc.o
from source instead:

  cd tools/tcc
  git clone https://github.com/TinyCC/tinycc.git
  rm -rf tinycc/.git

Then edit tanja-tcc-main.c (the TanjaOS void main(char*) entry point that
wraps tcc's real main - see the comment at the top of the file) and rebuild
with the normal top-level build:

  x86:    make           (from the repo root)
  arm64:  make -f Makefile.arm64

Once tinycc/tcc.c exists, make detects it and runs build-tcc.sh (or
build-tcc-arm64.sh, via TCC_BUILD_SCRIPT) automatically - no separate step
needed. Those scripts also generate tinycc/config.h and tinycc/tccdefs_.h
on first run (normally produced by tinycc's own Makefile via ./configure
and its c2str tool); this only needs a host cc on your build machine and
happens once, so it works the same on the Pi as on x86.

Requirements: x86 build needs gcc -m32 (gcc-multilib) and binutils; arm64
(cross) build needs i686-linux-gnu-gcc and i686-linux-gnu-binutils. Either
way a host cc is also needed for the one-time header generation above.

If you change tanja-libc.c's function set, check nm -u tcc.o afterwards -
every undefined symbol has to be one the kernel exports (kernel/elf.c);
anything else won't link at runtime and TanjaOS has no linker to tell you.
